gpui: Attach a macOS WebView below GPUI's overlays, above base content

Against longbridge/nsh e626a8d (0.4.0), after patches 0001-0003. `WebItem`
built a WebView as a plain child of the window (`build_as_child(&*window)`),
so AppKit always drew it above everything GPUI itself paints there: every
menu, popover, dialog and toast had to hide or placeholder-swap the WebView
while open (the app's `nativeOcclusion.ts` workaround).

On macOS, `WebItem::build` now attaches the WebView through
`window.enable_window_composition().create_native_surface()`: the mechanism
GPUI itself uses to mix native and GPUI-drawn content in one window
(`crates/gpui/nsh-platform-gpui/src/native/web.rs`, new `mod macos`, mirroring
the existing Linux `mod linux`). gpui-fast-macos's `register_platform_surface`
(`fast/composition.rs`, read only; not patched) always places a newly created
native surface below the window's `overlay_surface`, so anything GPUI paints
into that overlay surface with `Window::with_composition_surface` stays
visible over the page, and AppKit's own `hitTest:` already falls through an
uncovered `GPUIOverlayView` to the plain container view behind it
(`overlay_hit_test`): no vendor patch to gpui-fast or lb-wry, and no hit-test
override, was needed.

nsh's own popups (menus, dialogs, toasts; `position: fixed` promotion,
`core/overlay.rs` `sync_overlays`) carry no bit on the wire marking them as
overlays. `native::prepaint` computes, every frame, the highest index among
the surface root's children that a currently shown web view descends from
(`root_child_index`), caching it on `Surface::overlay_split` (`None` with no
web view shown). `render.rs`'s `SurfaceElement::paint` gets the window's
overlay surface id once, up front, from that index; `DrawCx::route_from`
makes the main pass skip drawing every root child after the split (one
comparison added to `draw_content`'s shared, recursive children loop,
checked only when the view being drawn is the surface's root), and
`draw_overlay_children` draws those children once, into that surface,
afterward -- not a double paint of that content when only one web view
shares the window. `overlay_hitboxes` registers a `BlockMouse` hitbox per
routed child there too, for AppKit's own `hitTest:` (skipped for a child
whose own `pointer_events` is `"none"`, so a full-window toast or tooltip
wrapper styled that way does not make the page dead everywhere else). With
no web view shown, or the composition call failing, nothing is skipped or
routed and a window renders exactly as before this patch (never silently
dropping content).

Routing a hitbox into `overlay_surface` has a side effect: gpui-fast-macos's
composition-aware `hit_test` stops at the first surface, front to back, that
covers a point, so once an overlay hitbox or a native web view surface
covers a point, `events.rs`'s checks against the surface's own single,
whole-window hitbox (always in the base surface) stop seeing that point as
hovered, breaking nsh's own click/scroll/hover dispatch to the very content
just routed there. `render.rs`'s `overlay_hitboxes` now also keeps the
`Hitbox` handles on `Surface::overlay_input_hitboxes`, and `events.rs` grew a
`hovered_at` helper, used at every site that gated on the surface hitbox's
hover (mouse down, move, pinch, the cursor, and approximately the wheel),
that also checks those.

`Command::Blur` no longer calls wry's `focus_parent()` directly on macOS: it
would now target the composition surface's plain container view, which never
accepts first responder. It instead marks a `pending_blur` flag (and asks for
a repaint) that the next `prepaint` resolves with
`crate::chrome::take_first_responder`, the call patch 0002 already uses for a
GPUI-content mouse down. `WebItem::close()`/`Built`'s `Drop` release the
composition surface through a spawned window update
(`macos::NativeSurface::release`, mirroring the Linux module's `release`), so
repeated webview create/destroy cycles do not leak a tree registration.

A companion app-side fix was needed for any of this to be observable at all:
`t3ui/nsh.json`'s `capabilities.process.env` did not list
`T3GPUI_WEBVIEW_UNDER_SURFACE`, so nsh's own `process.env` (gated per name by
that capability list, `crates/nsh-core/src/runtime/process.rs`) silently
omitted it even though the OS-level environment variable reached the
process; `nativeOcclusion.ts`'s flag check always read `undefined`, so the
occlusion registry kept hiding web views exactly as before, no matter what
this patch did. Added to the capability list.

NOT included here, after reverting: a `T3GPUI_DUMP_HITTEST` runtime
hit-test/view-hierarchy dump and a `CATransaction`/`setDisableActions`
wrapper around `NativeSurface::place`'s frame changes (requirement 2,
smoothness), both gated behind new `objc2`/`objc2-foundation` dependencies.
Adding those two dependencies and this code reliably reproduced a window
that never painted past its initial black frame (stuck titled "nsh"),
confirmed against a same-moment stock-host control on the same machine,
which rendered normally; not root-caused before this patch had to ship
without them. See third_party/nsh-host/README.md for what was and was not
verified, and exactly which requirements pass and fail.

Only `crates/gpui/nsh-platform-gpui` changes (`native.rs`, `native/web.rs`,
`render.rs`, `surface.rs`, `events.rs`). GPUI itself is not patched. No
`Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` changes remain in this version of the patch (the
`objc2` additions were reverted along with the code that needed them).

